@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-applypatch-is-atomic-non-mutating-and-strict-about-rfc-6902-edge-cases
+type: decision-log
+title: "applyPatch is atomic, non-mutating and strict about RFC 6902 edge cases"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Services patch stored documents with `applyPatch`; RFC 6902 leaves some edge cases open.
+
+## Decision
+
+- The input document and patch are never mutated: the work is done on a deep clone, and values taken from the patch are cloned, so the result shares no structure with the inputs.
+- The patch is atomic: any invalid patch or failing operation throws and yields no result.
+- Missing or non-string `path`/`from`, a missing `value` where required, an unknown `op` and a non-array patch are invalid.
+- `add` accepts index `0..length` and `-` on arrays; `remove`, `replace`, `move` and `copy` sources require an existing target (no `-`, no index equal to the length).
+- Removing the whole document throws; `move` into its own child throws; `move` onto itself is a no-op.
+- `test` uses deep equality ignoring object key order; numbers compare by value, types are strict.
+- Members are written as own properties, so `__proto__` never touches the prototype.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Callers can retry or discard safely on error. The strict reading follows `resolvePointer` (dl-001).
